#h(1em)The story begins on the Connecticut River. In the middle of the 1800s the river ran
through farm country all the way down to Long Island Sound, and at its mouth stood New
London, one of the great whaling ports of the world. For a time only New Bedford sent more
ships after whales. Whale oil lit the lamps of America, and sperm oil, which burned clean
and ran fine and slick, greased its machines. Dad put it more grandly: whaling "actually
encouraged the Industrial Revolution by being able to oil gears and so forth."
// src: context; [S5 00:05:17]; [S5 00:07:26]
// context: New London the second-largest whaling port in the mid-1800s — https://www.nlchs.org/online-exhibits/whaling-out-of-new-london/
// context: sperm oil burned cleanly, a superior lubricant — https://www.whalingmuseum.org/research/research-resources/whaling-history/yankee-whaling/

Up the river, in South Windsor, on April 28, 1834, Erastus Williams Burnham and his wife,
Emeline, had a son and gave him his father's name. The family would call him Erasthus; his
wife, one day, would call him Bill. It was farm country, and farm work was what a boy could
look forward to. Dad understood the appeal of the alternative. "Say you're 12, 14 years
old and you want to do something," he said. "And all you have to look for is drudgery on a
farm."
// src: [S5 00:40:39]; [S5 00:07:36]; record (born Apr 28 1834, South Windsor; parents Erastus Williams Burnham and Emeline Parsons Burnham; called "Bill" by his wife)

#plate("/photos/print/P038-withheld.png", caption: "Erasthus Burnham, 1864", width: 3.4in)
// photo: P038 — AI illustration generated from the museum tintype (2004.09.013); see IMAGE-STYLE.md

#sectionbreak

#plate("/photos/print/P036-withheld.png", caption: "A New England whaleship", width: 4.6in)
// photo: P036 — supplied by the author; not identified as the Hannibal; baked-in caption cropped off

In the summer of 1851 he went to sea. On June 21 a clerk at New London wrote him onto the
crew list of the whaleship _Hannibal_ as "Erastus W. Brunham," of South Windsor, eighteen
years old, five feet six, light complexion, light hair. The _Hannibal_ was a ship of 441
tons, built in New York in 1821 and so already thirty years old, owned by Benjamin Brown's
Sons of New London. Her captain was Jeremiah Lester Jr., a Waterford man of twenty-eight.
Of the thirty-one men on the list, ten had been born in the Azores. Three days later she
sailed for the North Pacific. Whaleships were provisioned for up to four years, and by then
the average voyage lasted forty-six months: out through the Atlantic, around Cape Horn or
the Cape of Good Hope, and up into the Pacific to wherever the whales were. Which cape the
_Hannibal_ took, no surviving record says.
// src: record (at 17 went to sea; letters from the Ship Hannibal, June–August 1851); crew list AC061341 (Hannibal 1851–54, 1851 Jun 21: "Erastus W. Brunham", South Windsor, 18, 5'6", light, light; 31 men, 10 born "Western Islands"); voyage AV06134 (departed 1851 Jun 24, master Jeremiah Lester Jr., Waterford, 28; agent Benjamin F. Brown's Sons); vessel AS1529 (441 tons, built New York 1821) — https://whalinghistory.org/?s=AV06134 ; [S5 00:08:38]-[S5 00:09:43]
// context: provisioned for up to four years; voyages averaged 46 months by 1851 — https://www.nps.gov/nebe/learn/historyculture/whaleship.htm
// NOTE: two whaleships were named Hannibal; this is the New London ship (AS1529), not the
// Sag Harbor one (AS1530). See facts/records/erastus-burnham/hannibal.md.

#plate("/photos/print/P043.png", caption: "The voyage of the Hannibal, 1851–1854", width: 6.2in)
// map: P043 — drawn from the recorded positions in facts/records/erastus-burnham/hannibal_1851_54_track.csv (scripts/make_hannibal_map.py)

A green hand lived in the forecastle, a narrow three-cornered room under the deck in the bow,
lined with bunks. One young man who shipped out a few years earlier found his "black and
slimy with filth, very small, and as hot as an oven," and full of "tainted meat, Portuguese
ruffians, and sea-sick Americans." The men stood watch four hours on and four off. They ate
salt meat, hard biscuit and molasses; the same writer noted the "well-fed cockroaches in my
molasses" and allowed that they sometimes gave it a rich flavor. The high point of the week
was duff, a boiled flour pudding, and the men sang about it at the windlass.
// context: forecastle; rations — New Bedford Whaling Museum, "Life Aboard" — https://www.whalingmuseum.org/research/research-resources/whaling-history/life-aboard/
// context: J. Ross Browne, Etchings of a Whaling Cruise (1846), p. 24 (forecastle), p. 109 (cockroaches in the molasses), pp. 133–134 (duff chanty) — https://archive.org/details/whalingcruise00browrich
// context: Francis Allyn Olmsted, Incidents of a Whaling Voyage (1841), pp. 47–48 (two watches, four hours at a time; Olmsted sailed from New London) — https://archive.org/details/incidentsofwhali00olms

The first stop was the Azores, the Western Islands, where American whalers took on fresh
food and, often, fresh men. Erasthus wrote home in July and August 1851, from the island of
Flores. Trade there ran on barter; a few years earlier a whaler at Flores had bought three
thousand onions, two hundred eggs and four dozen cabbages for a single barrel of oil. On
August 20, while the _Hannibal_ was in those waters, a sperm whale in the Atlantic smashed a
whaleboat of the New Bedford ship _Ann Alexander_ "to fragments no larger than a common
chair" and then sank the ship. Two months later a New York writer named Herman Melville
published a very long novel about a whale that sank a ship. _Moby-Dick_ came out in London in
October and New York in November, sold poorly, and earned its author, in the end, \$556.37.
// src: record (Letters From the Sea nos. 2–3: 1851 Jul 24 & 25 refs "Island of Flora"; 1851 Aug 17 refs "Western Islands")
// context: Azores as first foreign stop; provisioning and recruiting; Flores 1847, a barrel of oil for "3,000 onions, 200 eggs & 4 dozen cabbages" — NPS ethnography, ch. 9 — https://home.nps.gov/nebe/learn/historyculture/upload/NEBE_Ethnographic_1825-1925_FY22_508.pdf
// context: Ann Alexander, Aug 20 1851 — Alexander Starbuck, History of the American Whale Fishery (1878), p. 119 — https://archive.org/details/historyofamerica00star
// context: Moby-Dick London Oct 18, New York Nov 14 1851; Melville's earnings $556.37 — https://blog.loa.org/2010/10/october-18-1851-melvilles-moby-dick-is.html

For the _Hannibal_, wherever the whales were meant the far north. Three years earlier a Sag
Harbor captain named Thomas Roys had taken his ship through the Bering Strait and found the
vast herds of bowhead whales, and the fleet had followed him: fifty ships in 1849, and in
1852, the peak, two hundred and twenty-four. A bowhead could yield more than a hundred and
fifty barrels of oil and a ton or more of baleen, the springy "bone" that stiffened corsets
and hoop skirts. In the Arctic summer the sun barely set, and the first whale ever taken
there was killed at midnight. In fog the ships called to one another with bells, guns, horns
and by pounding on empty casks. Not all of them came home. That July a New London ship, the
_Superior_, went ashore on the Siberian coast in thick fog while boiling out a whale. On
August 18 the _Hannibal_ was spoken with thirteen whales to her credit, and on August 31
another whaler logged her at sixty-nine degrees north, above the Bering Strait.
// src: The Polynesian (Honolulu), 1852 Oct 2, p. 2 (Hibernia's report: "18 [Aug.] Hannibal, do. [N.L.] 13 wb"; Superior "ashore in thick fog, trying out at the time") — https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn82015408/1852-10-02/ed-1/?sp=2
// src: CoML logbook data, Betsey Williams (AV01853), 1852 Aug 31, spoke Hannibal at 68.97 N, 172.43 W (facts/records/sources/hannibal/data/…coml_spoken…)
// context: Roys 1848 — Bockstoce, Arctic 37:4 (1984), p. 528 — https://pubs.aina.ucalgary.ca/arctic/arctic37-4-528.pdf ; fleet 50 (1849) to 224 (1852) — Northern Mariner 16:2, p. 55 — https://www.cnrs-scrn.org/northern_mariner/vol16/tnm_16_2_53-68.pdf
// context: bowhead of 150 bbl "with bone in proportion" — Starbuck p. 99n; first Arctic whale "captured at 12 o'clock at night"; fog signals — Starbuck pp. 98–99; baleen for corsets and hoop skirts — https://www.nps.gov/nebe/learn/historyculture/whaleproducts.htm
// NOTE: "a ton or more of baleen" is conservative against Scammon p. 52 (over 3,500 lb in the largest).

The work was filthy. A whale was killed from a small boat, towed alongside and cut up on
deck, and the blubber, sliced into what the men called Bible leaves, was boiled down in the
try-works, great iron pots set in brick amidships, whose fires at night made the ship look as
if she were burning. The men fried their biscuit in the boiling oil, and made fritters from
the whale's brains when the steward was feeling generous. The rest --- the offal, the rot,
the smell --- had to be lived with. "So the main thing it took," Dad said, "was a good
iron stomach." The pay was worse. A green hand's share, or lay, was about one two-hundredth
of the catch, which worked out to around twenty cents a day, when a laborer ashore made
ninety; and a man could finish four years at sea owing the ship for the clothes he had bought
from it.
// src: [S5 00:05:17]
// context: Browne (1846), pp. 61–63 (try-works "as if enveloped in flames"; "Bible leaves"; biscuit fried in oil; brain fritters)
// context: Elmo P. Hohman, The American Whaleman (1928), p. 15 (green hand's lay 1/200), p. 240 (about 20¢ a day vs about 90¢ for unskilled labor ashore) — https://archive.org/details/americanwhaleman00hohm
// context: debts to the slop chest — https://www.nps.gov/nebe/learn/historyculture/whaleship.htm

#plate("/photos/print/P039-withheld.png", caption: "A good iron stomach: the Arctic, 1852", width: 4.4in)
// photo: P039 — AI illustration generated from the museum tintype (2004.09.013); see IMAGE-STYLE.md

It was dangerous as well as filthy. On September 29 Erasthus wrote to his parents from what
he called the Kamchatka Sea, and his letter names the ship _Vesper_ of New London and her
captain, Edward Howes, whom the family knew as Captain House. Howes came from Glastonbury,
the next town down the river from South Windsor, and on July 15 that summer he had been
killed by a whale. The Kamchatka Sea was Russian water; Alaska itself would stay Russian
until 1867, when the United States bought it for \$7.2 million. The whalers sailed past the
Pribilof Islands, where, more than a century later, Dad would spend three years of his own.
// src: record (Letters From the Sea no. 4: 1852 Sep 29 to his parents from the "Kamchatka Sea"; refs "Captain House", "Ship Vesper", Arctic Ocean, Bering Strait); [S5 00:10:19]-[S5 00:11:09]
// context: Vesper of New London (AV15129), master Edward M. Howes, b. Glastonbury CT 1818, killed by a whale Jul 15 1852 — https://whalinghistory.org/?s=AV15129
// context: Alaska Purchase, treaty Mar 30 1867, $7.2 million — https://guides.loc.gov/alaska-treaty
// REVIEW: that the letter reports Howes's death is inference from the museum's index terms; the letter text has not been read.

#sectionbreak

In the autumn the fleet came south. On November 20, 1852, the _Hannibal_ anchored at Ronkiti
harbor on Ascension Island, as the whalers called Pohnpei, in the Caroline Islands. It was a
favorite stop, for the island chief's hospitality and for its prices: a hog cost five dollars
in tobacco. It was also a favorite place to jump ship. Some hundred and fifty beachcombers
lived ashore, deserters and castaways mostly, and one recaptured runaway had made his second
escape by paddling ashore in the captain's wooden bathtub. The first American missionaries had
arrived ten weeks before the _Hannibal_ and been unnerved by the rowdy beachcombers who met
their ship begging for tobacco. In January the _Hannibal_ left for Hong Kong, and on February
4, 1853, she arrived there with three other American whalers.
// src: The Polynesian, 1853 May 14, p. 3 ("Arrivals at Ascension Island, 1852 … Nov. 20. Hannibal"); 1853 Nov 19, p. 2 (Ronkiti Harbor list: "Nov 20, '52, Hannibal … Jan 13, '53 … for Hongkong"; OCR poor); 1853 Jun 4, p. 2 (Hong Kong: arrived "4th [Feb.], wh ship Hannibal, Lester" with Monongahela, Bart Gosnold, John Wells) — https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn82015408/1853-06-04/ed-1/?sp=2
// context: David Hanlon, Upon a Stone Altar: A History of the Island of Pohnpei to 1890 (1988), pp. 74–76 (Rohnkiti; hog $5 in tobacco; ~150 beachcombers; the bathtub), pp. 92–93 (ABCFM missionaries arrived Sep 6 1852; "rowdy group of beachcombers") — https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/e02874d4-99dc-40d6-865d-5cf44dc18c69/content

Hong Kong, as Dad explained, was a common stop for the whalers: it was British, so money
could be changed and stores taken on. It was there that Erasthus and the _Hannibal_ parted
company. The family's version is that the crew, unhappy with their treatment, mutinied.
Dad guessed it was the quiet kind: they went ashore for supplies and never went back. "But
that's something that could be seen from his letters," he said. If so, he had plenty of
company. The average whaleship of the time lost nearly two-thirds of the crew she sailed with
before she got home, and about three men in ten deserted.
// src: [S5 00:09:43]; [S5 00:13:20]; record (1852 letter refs Hong Kong; police force in Hong Kong, 1853); The Polynesian 1853 Jun 4 p. 2 (Hannibal at Hong Kong Feb 4 1853)
// context: Hohman (1928), p. 64 (turnover nearly two-thirds; about three in ten deserted)
// REVIEW: that he deserted is inferred from his leaving the ship at Hong Kong; the family calls
// it a mutiny. The author to confirm the wording.

Hong Kong in 1853 was a raw place to land. Britain had taken it from China only eleven years
before, and the colony's police were, by the force's own later account, "chaotic,
disorganised and made up of largely suspect individuals." Pirates worked the harbor and
burglars the streets at night. A stranded Yankee sailor who could stand up straight was,
evidently, police material, and Erasthus joined the force: a British bobby, as Dad put it.
// src: [S5 00:00:30]; record (served on the police force in Hong Kong, 1853)
// context: Treaty of Nanking Aug 29 1842 — https://www.britannica.com/event/Treaty-of-Nanjing
// context: Police Ordinance May 1 1844; early force "chaotic, disorganised and made up of largely suspect individuals"; piracy and night burglary — https://www.police.gov.hk/info/doc/history/chapter01_en.pdf

One day on patrol, as the family told it, seven bandits jumped him. They stabbed him seven
times in the back and threw him off a bridge. He lived, and years later he could still
show his grandson the scars. It was the day on which everything after it depended. "If he
hadn't made it through this," Dad said, "none of us would exist."
// src: [S5 00:00:30]-[S5 00:01:49]
// REVIEW: the museum confirms the Hong Kong police service; the stabbing is the family's
// account (via Grandfather Krug).

#plate("/photos/print/P040-withheld.png", caption: "Seven bandits and a bridge: Hong Kong, 1853", width: 4.4in)
// photo: P040 — AI illustration generated from the museum tintype (2004.09.013); see IMAGE-STYLE.md

The _Hannibal_ went on without him. In October 1853 she was at Lahaina, on Maui, a whaling
town with a brand-new prison for sailors, where the newspaper noted that she had "sailed
without clearance," which is to say without waiting for her papers; smallpox was loose in
the islands that autumn, and there were reasons not to linger. She reached New London on
April 4, 1854, with about half the oil of her previous voyage. Captain Lester died six years
later, in command of another New London ship, and in 1861 the _Hannibal_ herself was
abandoned in Cumberland Inlet, off Baffin Island.
// src: The Polynesian 1853 Oct 29 p. 2 (Port of Lahaina: "sh Hannibal, Lester, sailed without clearance") — https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn82015408/1853-10-29/ed-1/?sp=2 ; voyage AV06134 (returned Apr 4 1854: 97 sperm, 1,678 whale, 20,000 bone; previous voyage 100 sperm, 3,400 whale); vessel AS1529 ("Aband, Cumberland Inlet, 1861"); Lester: master of the Pioneer, died Jun 14 1860 (AV07659)
// context: Hale Paʻahao, Lahaina's prison, built 1852–53 — https://www.lahainarestoration.org/lahainaprison.html ; smallpox epidemic of 1853 (The Polynesian 1853 Dec 17 p. 2)
// NOTE: "without waiting for her papers" is the plain meaning of the phrase; no source explains this case.

#sectionbreak

By 1855 he was home and working as a moulder in a brass foundry in Holyoke, Massachusetts, a
new city built a few years earlier to harness the Connecticut River, and on May 15 of that
year he married Mary Graham Devine, a Scot. Dad had remembered him marrying at about
twenty-one, and he was right. The sea still had a hold on him. In 1857 and 1858 he was
writing home to Mary from the sloop _Mary Elizabeth_, working the coast and the rivers, from
the Hudson to Fall River. Then he settled back in South Windsor and worked
in the paper mills there. Three of the children he and Mary had would die as babies, in 1856,
1864 and 1868.
// src: record (brass-foundry moulder in Holyoke; married Mary Graham Devine May 15 1855; letters from the Sloop Mary Elizabeth 1857–58: Hudson River, Middletown, Norwich, Fall River; South Windsor paper mills, Wood & Keeney and Parsons; children William 1856, Henry E. 1864, Robert L. 1868 died in infancy); [S5 00:21:16]
// context: Holyoke begun 1847 as a planned industrial city — https://www.holyoke.org/history-of-holyoke/

#sectionbreak

On December 28, 1861, eight months into the Civil War, Erasthus enlisted at Hartford as a
private in Company A of the 12th Connecticut Infantry. Company A called itself the Colt
Guards, after the Hartford gun works, and it was mostly Hartford men, with six from South
Windsor. The regiment's roll for 1862 sums him up in four words: twenty-seven, married,
paper-maker. The regiment spent an unusually cold and stormy winter under canvas at Camp
Lyon, with stoves in the tents and measles going round, and at the end of February it marched
out over fresh snow, still shivering, to board the steamship _Fulton_ at New York.
// src: record (letters list); Record of Service of Connecticut Men (1889), 12th C.V. reprint, p. 6: "Burnham, Erastus W. Hartford, Dec. 28,'61"; Catalogue of the 12th & 13th Regiments (1862), Co. A: "Burnham, Erastus W. … 27 Married, Paper-maker."
// context: Croffut & Morris, Military and Civil History of Connecticut (1868), pp. 142–145 ("Colt Guards"; "Hartford, thirty-six; South Windsor, six"; stoves; "unusually cold and stormy"; measles; marched out after a snow, "still shivering") — facts/records/erastus-burnham/civil_war/context_civil_war.md §1
// NOTE: sailing date Feb 27 (1889 reprint) vs Mar 1 (C&M); text says "end of February" for the march-out only.

Eight quiet days later the _Fulton_ put them ashore at Ship Island, off the Mississippi coast,
which one of the 12th's own captains called the sandiest region this side of the Great
Sahara. The men got drinking water by sinking barrels two feet into the sand and waiting.
Erasthus's first letters to Mary came from there, and from the ship _E. W. Farley_ anchored
off it, and in one of them he named three men of a Maine regiment who had drowned. In
April the _Farley_ carried the 12th into the mouth of the Mississippi. The men watched the
navy bombard Fort Jackson and dodged the fire-ships the rebels floated down on them; one
night a sunken gunboat stove in the _Farley_'s side and tipped the men out of their berths.
Erasthus turned twenty-eight on the river, and wrote to Mary on his birthday.
// src: record (letters: Ship Island Mar–Apr 1862; "Ship Wilder Farley at Ship Island", Apr 3 and 5; Apr 10 names three drowned men of the 12th Maine, Co. B; Apr 28 1862 from the Mississippi, names Fort Jackson and Porter)
// context: C&M p. 145 ("a quiet passage of eight days"; "the low sand-beach of Ship Island"); pp. 159–160 (bombardment of Fort Jackson; fire-ships; the midnight crash, the Farley "careened so as to throw the men out of their berths")
// context: Capt. J. W. De Forest, 12th C.V., "the sandiest region this side of the Great Sahara"; barrels sunk in the sand — Hollandsworth, Mississippi History Now, via context_civil_war.md §2
// REVIEW: the three drowned 12th Maine men are named only in the museum's letter index; no other account found.

Farragut's fleet had run past the forts and taken New Orleans, and on the evening of April 30
the 12th became the first Union regiment to appear off the city, a day ahead of anyone else.
They slept that night on a wharf. New Orleans did not want them. White citizens glowered at
the troops from the levee; a boy asked by a Connecticut sailor to drop a rope over a post
replied, "No, I'll be damned if I will!"; and within two weeks General Benjamin Butler had
issued his order that any woman who insulted a Union soldier would be treated as "a woman of
the town plying her avocation," which caused a diplomatic incident with Britain. The 12th's
colonel, Henry Deming, was made mayor of the city.
// src: record (letters: May 25 1862 names Deming and Washington)
// context: C&M p. 160 ("arriving off New Orleans on the evening of April 30, a day before any other troops"; bivouacked on a wharf), p. 161 (the glowering citizens; "No, I'll be damned if I will!"), p. 307 (Deming appointed Mayor of New Orleans)
// context: General Order No. 28, May 15 1862; Palmerston's protest — https://hnoc.org/research-collections/collection-highlights/broadside-depicting-benjamin-butler-e2-80-99s-general-order-no-28
// context: Record of Service (1889), 12th reprint, p. 1: "the first Union regiment to appear" (via regiment_12th_ct_timeline.csv)

In May the regiment went twelve miles upriver to Camp Parapet, an abandoned rebel camp whose
guns had been spiked by the women of the neighborhood. There was drill every morning and
drill every afternoon, the camp was terribly muddy, and in the later summer typhoid made, in
the words of the state's history, "fearful havoc." Sometimes a hundred men were in the
hospital at once, and more than forty died. Company A was sent downriver to Jefferson City
for June and July, with its captain as provost marshal, and it was from there that
Erasthus's last Louisiana letters were written. The state's official roster records his
discharge for disability on July 29, 1862, the same day as four other privates of his
company. His last letter from Louisiana is dated August 15, and he was home by the end of
the summer.
// src: record (Wood Memorial letters list: Camp Parapet May–Jul; Jefferson City Jul 26, Aug 15 1862, note "discharged on disability end of summer 1862")
// src: Record of Service (1889), 12th C.V. reprint, p. 6: "Disc. dis. July 29, '62. (See Priv. Co. A, 1st C. V. H. A.)"; same date for Co. A privates H. E. Bidwell, J. Carlan, J. N. Gilbert, G. McCann (pp. 6–7)
// context: C&M p. 160 (Camp Parapet "twelve miles above the city"; guns "spiked … by the women of the neighborhood"; drills), p. 306 (mud; typhoid; "Sometimes a hundred were in the hospital at once. More than forty died"; "Company A was at Jefferson City in June and July, Capt. Lewis provost-marshal")
// REVIEW: the museum gives the discharge as "officially Jan. 14, 1863"; the 1889 official roster
// says July 29, 1862. The chapter follows the roster. See facts/gaps.md §7.

#sectionbreak

He went back to the paper mills, and for a year and a half the war went on without him. Then,
in the autumn of 1863, President Lincoln called for three hundred thousand more men, with a
draft to follow in any town that had not filled its quota by January 5, 1864. Connecticut
met the call with money: a new recruit could now collect, from the state and the federal
government together, six hundred and two dollars. On January 5, 1864, the last day before the draft, Erasthus enlisted again, at
South Windsor. The state's own historians later judged that the bounty system "filled the
quota without filling the army." It did fill Company A of the 1st Connecticut Heavy
Artillery with at least one man who already knew how to be a soldier.
// src: Record of Service (1889), 1st C.V.H.A. reprint, p. 9: "Burnham, Erastus W. So. Windsor, Jan. 5,'64"
// context: C&M p. 459 (Lincoln's call of Oct 17 1863; draft if quotas not filled "by Jan. 5, 1864"), pp. 459–460, 629 ($300 state bounty, Act of Nov 13 1863; total "six hundred and two dollars for new levies"), p. 636 ("filled the quota without filling the army")
// NOTE: that the Jan 5 date and the draft deadline are connected is inference; the text states the coincidence only.

The 1st Connecticut Heavy Artillery ran the siege guns for the armies closing on Richmond. It
had been drilled on every kind of gun and mortar the army owned, and it was proud to say it
was inferior to no regular regiment in the service. Company A spent the winter at Fort
Reynolds, in the defenses of Alexandria, and Erasthus spent it writing to Mary about
Copperheads and the governor and the parcels he was sending home by Adams Express. In May
the regiment went to Bermuda Hundred to join General Butler, Erasthus's old commander from
New Orleans, whose drive on Richmond had just been stopped cold at Drewry's Bluff. He wrote to
Mary on May 15, their ninth wedding anniversary, from "Camp in the Wilderness," and three
days later from the front line. Through the early summer Company A served the guns at
Battery No. 6 and at Spring Hill.
// src: record (letters: Fort Reynolds Jan–Mar 1864, Copperheads Feb 7, Gov. Buckingham, Adams Express Jan 26 and Feb 15; May 15 1864 "Camp in the Wilderness"; May 18 "from the front line", names Fort Darling; Jun 8 "No. 6 Battery"; Jul 15 Spring Hill)
// context: Record of Service (1889), 1st C.V.H.A. reprint, p. 2 ("inferior neither in discipline nor efficiency to any regular regiment in service"; to Butler May 1864); Taylor & Hatfield, History of the First Connecticut Artillery (1893), p. 47 (Co. A, Fort Reynolds, Jan–Apr 1864), p. 67 (Abbot, Jul 8 1864: "I have taken Co. A from battery No. 6"; second platoon of Co. A to Spring Hill)
// context: Drewry's Bluff, May 16 1864 — https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va053

#plate("/photos/print/P042-withheld.png", caption: "Company A, 1st Connecticut Heavy Artillery: Petersburg, 1864", width: 4.6in)
// photo: P042 — AI illustration generated from the museum tintype (2004.09.013); see IMAGE-STYLE.md

Then came Petersburg, and the siege. At a quarter to five on the morning of July 30, 1864,
Union miners fired the powder they had packed into a tunnel dug more than five hundred feet
under the Confederate line, and left a hole in it a hundred and seventy feet long and thirty
feet deep. The
infantry went into the crater and could not get out again. The regiment's guns, eighty-one of
them, fired 3,833 rounds that morning. Company A's two batteries of 8-inch mortars, under
Captain Gillett and Lieutenant Patterson, fired 486 of them. The regiment's famous 13-inch
mortar on its railroad car, the _Dictator_, which weighed seventeen thousand pounds and
broke the first car it was fired from, managed nineteen. It belonged to another company.
That night the regiment moved fifty-two heavy guns seven miles without the enemy noticing.
// src: record (letter of Oct 30 1864 from Fort Morton names "The Crater" and Capt. Gillette)
// context: Taylor & Hatfield (1893), pp. 84–85 (mine sprung 4:45 a.m.; Gillett's four 8-inch mortars 269 rounds, Patterson's six 8-inch mortars 217; regiment 3,833 rounds from 81 guns; big mortar 19; 52 guns moved, "The rebels did not discover the movement"), pp. 68–69, 103 (the Dictator, 17,000 lb; "The first car broke under the shock"; Co. G's gun)
// context: Record of Service (1889), 1st C.V.H.A. reprint, pp. 3–4 (Crater Jul 30, Companies A, B, C, D, F, G, I, K, M)
// context: crater "170 feet long, 60 feet wide at either end and 30 feet deep in places" — https://www.nps.gov/places/the-crater.htm
// NOTE: tunnel length is given as 510, 511 and 586 ft in different sources, hence "more than five hundred feet". Powder charge left out (not on the NPS pages).

On August 17 Company A took four 10-inch mortars and two 4½-inch guns into Fort Morton, in
the lines facing the Crater, and stayed there through the winter. The magazines lay under six
feet of earth, the gunners worked behind rope mats six inches thick that weighed nearly six
hundred pounds apiece, and the Confederates across the way fired back with English Whitworth
guns, which Erasthus mentioned in a letter. In September he wrote from the regimental
hospital, which its commander called one of the best field hospitals he had ever seen. That
autumn his letters were full of the election, McClellan against Lincoln; Lincoln carried
Connecticut by 2,406 votes, and nearly three thousand of those counted came from soldiers in
the field. When the Confederates finally left their lines before dawn on April 3, 1865, the
regiment's history says, "for the First Connecticut Artillery the war was ended." Erasthus
wrote home from Broadway Landing on April 10, the day after Lee surrendered.
// src: record (letters: Fort Morton Sep–Oct 1864; Sep 17 1864 "Regt. Hospital"; Nov 28 1864 "Whitworth Battery", Cemetery Hill; Oct 16–Nov 15 McClellan and Lincoln; Apr 10 1865 Broadway Landing, names Lee)
// context: Taylor & Hatfield (1893), p. 88 (Aug 17 1864, Co. A to Fort Morton with four 10-inch mortars and two 4½-inch guns), p. 98 (Surgeon Skinner, "one of the best field hospitals I have ever seen"), p. 103 (rope mantlets "weighing nearly 600 pounds each"; magazines), p. 104 (Whitworth 12-pounders); Record of Service (1889), 1st C.V.H.A. reprint, p. 4 ("for the First Connecticut Artillery the war was ended")
// context: C&M pp. 636–637 (Lincoln's majority 2,406; 2,898 soldiers' votes)

The regiment spent the spring hauling away a hundred and seventy-six captured guns and
mortars, most of them heavy, and then went to Fort Lyon, near Alexandria, where it
test-fired 15-inch guns until, as its history puts it, "all danger of trouble with the
French army in Mexico was over." In September Company A went across the Potomac to garrison
Fort Foote, whose two Rodman guns weighed twenty-five tons each and threw a 440-pound shell
three and a half miles. He was mustered out on September 25, 1865. Two days later the
regiment took the train north; Philadelphia turned out at midnight to meet it and New York
jammed Broadway. Then it went up the Connecticut River by boat, past landings crowded with
what seemed the same people who had cheered it off to war four years before, and at half past
ten on the morning of September 29 the men came ashore at Colt's dock in Hartford. There was
a parade. "No speeches were made," the _Hartford Times_ reported, with evident approval.
// src: Record of Service (1889), 1st C.V.H.A. reprint, p. 9 ("M. o. Sep. 25, '65"); record (letters from Fort Lyon and Fort Foote)
// context: Record of Service (1889), 1st C.V.H.A. reprint, p. 4 (176 guns and mortars; 15-inch guns and the French army in Mexico); Taylor & Hatfield (1893), p. 163 (Companies K and A to Fort Foote, Sep 1865), pp. 165–166 (Alexandria Sep 27; Philadelphia at midnight; Broadway; up the river; Hartford Times: arrived 10:30, "landed at Colt's dock … No speeches were made"; parade)
// context: Fort Foote, two 15-inch Rodmans, 25 tons, 440-lb shell, range over 3½ miles — https://www.nps.gov/fofo/learn/historyculture/stories.htm

The family remembered his war differently. In their telling he was a surgeon, drafted for
his first term and paid for his second, to go in the place of a man who did not want to, and
the money bought the farm. The surgery Dad described was brutal: first aid, then a saw
through a man's leg, a shot of whiskey, and tar to seal the stump. He had seen the picture
books of the battlefields, and the evidence the war left walking around afterwards: "there
were all these peg-legged people." Whatever else Erasthus was, Dad said, he "had the guts
to do it." The letters and the regiment's records describe a soldier, and there was no Union
draft until 1863. The money, though, was real: it was the bounty that came with the second
enlistment. It is the
way of family stories to improve a little with each telling, and this one had fifty years
and two tellers to do it in.
// src: [S5 00:06:26]-[S5 00:07:07]; [S5 00:17:25]; [S5 00:20:01]; record (enlisted as a volunteer; no mention of surgeon or substitute in the catalogue)
// context: Union draft began with the Enrollment Act of Mar 3 1863 — https://www.archives.gov/publications/prologue/1994/winter/civil-war-draft-records.html
// REVIEW: Dad also says amputations were done without sedative and only one in ten
// survived; the record has anesthesia in ~95% of operations and ~70% survival. Left out.

#plate("/photos/print/P041-withheld.png", caption: "The surgeon's tent, as the family told it", width: 4.4in)
// photo: P041 — AI illustration generated from the museum tintype (2004.09.013); see IMAGE-STYLE.md

The war ran through the family on both sides. Dad's grandfather married a woman from
Midlothian, Virginia, whose own grandfather --- the name comes through as Khofer --- was an
officer for the South. The two of them, Dad said, were "fighting each other in the same
battlefield."
// src: [S5 00:18:40]-[S5 00:19:24]
// REVIEW: surname heard as Khofer/Kofor; left as heard per the glossary.

#sectionbreak

After the war Erasthus came home to South Windsor, bought a tobacco farm and ran it for
sixteen years. Then he went back to Holyoke and the paper mills. By 1883 he was foreman of
the engine room at the George R. Dickinson Paper Mill, and he belonged to the Grand Army of
the Republic, the Union veterans' society, and to the First Hose Company, one of the city's
volunteer fire companies.
// src: record (tobacco farm 16 years; Chemical Paper Co.; 1883 foreman of the engine room, George R. Dickinson Paper Mill; First Hose Company; GAR Kilpatrick Post No. 71, 1885–90)

The family remembered one more Holyoke job. The city ran on its canals, and in winter they
froze, and men went into them, drunk or fighting or simply unlucky, and stayed there until
the thaw, when the water was let down through the sluices and the bodies came with it.
Someone had to go in after them. Erasthus went, lowered on a rope in a little chair, because
nobody else could stand the stench. Whaling and war had left him with a stronger stomach than
most. Dad's verdict was brief: "Erastus was happy to do it."
// src: [S5 00:52:53]-[S5 00:53:50]
// REVIEW: not in the museum record; his fire-company membership is documented.

In 1899 he moved back to South Windsor for good and grew tobacco there for the rest of his
life. His grandson William Burnham Krug, born in Holyoke in 1894, was with him through his
old age, down at the shore at Old Lyme and out on the farm, and it was Krug who heard the
stories and saw the scars. Erasthus died at South Windsor on April 11, 1921, at eighty-six,
and was buried in the town's Center Cemetery.
// src: record (moved back 1899, farmed tobacco until his death; died Apr 11 1921; buried Center Cemetery, South Windsor); [S5 00:01:56]-[S5 00:02:32]; [S5 00:01:49]; [S5 00:24:00]

#sectionbreak

He left more behind than stories. His Civil War uniform and sword stood for years in the
living room of Dad's grandparents, next to a crank phonograph that Dad used to play as
a boy. They did not survive the family: "Oh, people in the family pillaged them, you know,"
Dad said. The letters did. Relatives gave them to the Wood Memorial Library and Museum in
South Windsor, more than seventy-five from the war and a handful from the sea, each with a
transcription. "People wrote a lot to each other then," Dad said, "even these wonderful
letters from the Civil War. Very eloquent and very good handwriting." With eight
grandchildren coming along, he meant to drive over one day with a cousin and scan them, so
that everybody could read them.
// src: [S5 00:16:28]-[S5 00:17:25]; [S5 00:03:20]-[S5 00:04:35]; [S5 00:14:50]-[S5 00:16:15]; [S5 00:21:48]-[S5 00:22:44]; record (75+ Civil War letters with transcriptions; letters from the sea)

What he made of it all was less about the man than about the thread. Any one of those
moments --- the bridge, the knife, the war --- might have ended it. "If he had died in any of
those incidents," he said, "none of us would have any existence." He would have liked to
look back in time and cheer him on, and he had a word for the man on the bridge: "Good thing
they didn't get your, whatever, your kidney or something."
// src: [S5 00:23:02]-[S5 00:23:51]

#sectionbreak

They didn't. Erasthus Burnham went to sea at seventeen, came home from the far side of the
world with seven scars, went to war twice, and lived to be eighty-six in the town where he
was born. His letters are still there, a few miles from his grave, in his own very good
handwriting, waiting for the family to come and read them.
// src: record (born and died South Windsor; letters at the Wood Memorial Library & Museum); [S5 00:04:35]

